<%# The ask at the end of a Verto: keep this one, by giving an address.
    Rendered only when the creator turned the block on (Survey#join_prompt?),
    and inert in owner preview and Test Mode like every other live endpoint —
    the controller's joinUrl value is blank there, and _renderJoinState never
    reveals the card.

    No locals, reading @survey and t("player.join_*") — the player-partial
    convention. The heading, body and button are the CREATOR'S sentences;
    Survey#join_title_text and friends fall back to the house copy when the
    field is blank, so this template never has to know which it got.

    Four states, all in this one card: ask (below), sent, and two failures.
    Only the ask is server-rendered — the rest are written by
    player_controller#_renderJoinState, because they carry the address the
    person typed and that never goes near the page cache. %>
<div class="join-card hidden" data-player-target="joinBlock">
  <div class="join-eyebrow"><%= t("player.join_eyebrow") %></div>
  <%# A div, not a heading: the player renders no h1/h2 anywhere (the thank-you
      title itself is .preview-thankyou-title on a div), so an <h3> here would
      be the page's only heading and would announce as a skipped level. %>
  <div class="join-title"><%= @survey.join_title_text %></div>
  <p class="join-body"><%= @survey.join_body_text %></p>

  <div class="join-ask" data-player-target="joinAsk">
    <%# COLLAPSED FIRST. The card opens on its pitch and one button; the form —
        Google, the two rows, the hint, the fine print — appears when the button
        is tapped. Measured on a 390×844 phone the full form made the end screen
        1127px, 283px of which a respondent had to scroll for, and 560px of that
        was this card; collapsed it fits with room. The cost is one tap, paid
        only by somebody who has just said they want the account.

        The reveal button carries the creator's CTA text, and so does the submit
        inside the form: it is the same intent ("Create my account"), met twice
        — once to say yes, once to send. Only one is ever visible. %>
    <div class="join-row join-row-cta" data-player-target="joinReveal">
      <button type="button" class="join-btn" aria-expanded="false"
              aria-controls="join-form-<%= @survey.id %>"
              data-action="click->player#joinExpand">
        <%= @survey.join_cta_text %>
      </button>
    </div>

    <div class="join-form" id="join-form-<%= @survey.id %>" hidden data-player-target="joinForm">
      <%# Other Vertos this browser still holds a durable key for. Hidden until
          the controller finds any, and checked by default: the person is being
          offered their own history, not opted into someone else's. %>
      <label class="join-also hidden" data-player-target="joinAlso">
        <input type="checkbox" checked data-player-target="joinAlsoBox">
        <span data-player-target="joinAlsoLabel"></span>
      </label>

      <%# Embedded in a third-party page, localStorage is partitioned or blocked
          outright, so this browser can see nothing it played elsewhere. Said
          plainly rather than silently offering less. %>
      <p class="join-note hidden" data-player-target="joinEmbedded"><%= t("player.join_embedded") %></p>

      <%# Continue with Google, above the address rows because it is the shorter
          road: no password to invent at the end of a survey, and an address
          Google has already verified, which is the one thing the emailed link
          was ever for.

          A button, not a form. OmniAuth's request phase is a CSRF-protected POST
          and this page is service-worker cached, so its token can be any age —
          the same fact that put #join under null_session. #joinGoogle posts the
          run's claims to a cookie-free endpoint and follows the /you/ URL it
          answers with, where the token is live. See PlayerJoinsController.

          Rendered only when the strategy is actually configured, so an
          unconfigured deploy shows the address form alone rather than a button
          that 404s. %>
      <% if SocialAuth.player_enabled? %>
        <button type="button" class="join-google" data-player-target="joinGoogleBtn"
                data-action="click->player#joinGoogle">
          <%= render "shared/google_mark" %>
          <span><%= t("auth.social_continue", provider: SocialAuth.label_for(:google_player)) %></span>
        </button>
        <div class="join-divider" aria-hidden="true"><span><%= t("auth.social_divider") %></span></div>
      <% end %>

      <%# Three rows, one thing each. The address and the password were once side
          by side — two 11rem fields fighting over a 430px column, and the
          password is the field people mistype. The button shared the password's
          row after that, which was the same mistake one step smaller. %>
      <div class="join-row">
        <input type="email" class="join-input"
               inputmode="email" autocomplete="email" enterkeyhint="next"
               spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off"
               placeholder="<%= t("player.join_email_placeholder") %>"
               aria-label="<%= t("player.join_email_placeholder") %>"
               data-player-target="joinEmail"
               data-action="keydown.enter->player#joinSubmit input->player#joinTyped">
    </div>

    <%# autocomplete="new-password" so a password manager offers to GENERATE
        one rather than filling whatever it has for this domain — this is a
        signup, and the same form doubles as a sign-in for a returning
        address. minlength mirrors Player::MIN_PASSWORD; the server enforces
        it either way, this is only so the browser says so first.

        Show/Hide, because this is the one field on the card with no way to
        check what you typed. It is a 12-character minimum invented at the end
        of a survey on a phone keyboard, and the cost of getting it wrong is
        not a retry — it is an account whose password is a typo, on a service
        with no respondent password reset. %>
    <div class="join-row">
      <div class="password-field join-password-field" <%= tag.attributes(data: password_visibility_data) %>>
        <input type="password" class="join-input"
               autocomplete="new-password" enterkeyhint="send"
               minlength="<%= Player::MIN_PASSWORD %>"
               placeholder="<%= t("player.join_password_placeholder") %>"
               aria-label="<%= t("player.join_password_placeholder") %>"
               data-player-target="joinPassword"
               data-password-visibility-target="input"
               data-action="keydown.enter->player#joinSubmit input->player#joinTyped">
        <%= render "shared/password_toggle" %>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="join-note"><%= t("player.join_password_hint", count: Player::MIN_PASSWORD) %></p>

    <%# The button gets a row of its own rather than sharing the password's.
        Beside a 12-character minimum and a Show toggle it left the field about
        236px wide, which is under half of what it takes to read back a
        passphrase — and the whole point of the toggle is reading it back. Full
        width also makes it unambiguously the primary action, which it is. %>
    <div class="join-row join-row-cta">
      <button type="button" class="join-btn" data-player-target="joinBtn"
              data-action="click->player#joinSubmit">
        <%= @survey.join_cta_text %>
      </button>
    </div>

    <p class="join-error hidden" role="alert" data-player-target="joinError"></p>
    <%# Promises the confirmation mail only where one can be sent: on a deploy
        with no working mail it would be a promise nothing keeps. A deploy-wide
        fact, so it is safe inside the shared cached_play_page bytes. %>
    <p class="join-fine"><%= t(MailConfigCheck.deliverable? ? "player.join_fine_confirm" : "player.join_fine") %></p>
    </div>

    <%# The door for somebody who already has an account.
        /you/sign-in has existed since passwords did, and until now nothing in
        the app linked to it: a returning respondent on a new phone was shown a
        button that says "Create my account" and left to guess that typing
        their old password into it would work. It does — the endpoint doubles
        as a sign-in — but "it happens to work if you don't believe what the
        button says" is not a door.

        Outside the card's flow and a plain link, because it is the way out for
        the few rather than the path for the many. A new tab always, not only
        when it matters: a Verto is routinely framed by a third party (see
        allow_embedding), where following this in place would replace somebody
        else's page with a sign-in form — and whether this render is embedded
        is something only the browser knows. %>
    <p class="join-alt">
      <%= t("player.join_have_account") %>
      <%= link_to t("player.join_sign_in"), new_player_session_path,
                  class: "join-alt-link", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>
    </p>
  </div>

  <%# Sent / failed. aria-live so the change is announced: the button that was
      just pressed disappears, and without this a screen reader is told
      nothing at all. %>
  <div class="join-done hidden" aria-live="polite" data-player-target="joinDone"></div>
</div>
